<Project>

  <!-- The pinned BepInEx build, shared with build-support.psm1. -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)BepInEx.props" />

  <!--
    Keep every generated file under one gitignored .build\ folder at the repo
    root, instead of scattering bin\ and obj\ through src\. The PowerShell build
    scripts put their staging and dist folders there too.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BuildRootDir>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory).build\</BuildRootDir>
    <BaseOutputPath>$(BuildRootDir)bin\$(MSBuildProjectName)\</BaseOutputPath>
    <BaseIntermediateOutputPath>$(BuildRootDir)obj\$(MSBuildProjectName)\</BaseIntermediateOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Where the build reads its BepInEx and Il2CppInterop reference assemblies
    from. Three projects need them - Persistence, Session and the Plugin - and
    resolving it here rather than in each csproj means one order, one cache and
    one error message for all of them.

    Resolution order, first hit wins:

      1. -p:DiscoElysiumDir=<path>   (a command-line property beats anything set here)
      2. the DISCO_ELYSIUM_DIR environment variable
      3. a modded copy kept in <repo>\.game_reference_copies
      4. the machine-level cache the PowerShell scripts write

    EVERY candidate is checked for BepInEx\core rather than merely for
    existing, and both halves of that matter:

      * A path that is simply gone must not win. The cache is a file naming a
        directory, and the directory can be deleted without the file noticing -
        which is exactly what happened when the repo's old reference install was
        removed, leaving a bare 'dotnet build' failing on a path that no longer
        existed while a perfectly good install sat elsewhere on the machine.
      * A PRISTINE copy must not win either. The reference copies in
        .game_reference_copies are mostly straight from Steam, and a game
        without BepInEx cannot supply a single one of the assemblies these
        projects reference. Checking for BepInEx\core is what tells the two
        kinds of copy apart.

    Step 4 is what makes git worktrees work: the cache is per-user and lives
    outside the repo (see $RefDirCacheFile in build-support.psm1), so one
    .\provision-refs.ps1 or .\build.ps1 anywhere on the machine answers the
    question for every checkout on it. It is also the only step that can find
    the Steam install, since MSBuild cannot read Steam's library folders -
    which makes the PowerShell scripts the way to recover when nothing here
    resolves.

    None of these values may be wrapped across lines: MSBuild trims only the
    ends of a property value, so a line break inside one lands in the middle of
    the path and every Exists() check on it then fails.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GameRefCopiesDir>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory).game_reference_copies\</GameRefCopiesDir>
    <RepoDefaultGameDir>$(GameRefCopiesDir)Steam Install - Unaltered\Disco Elysium</RepoDefaultGameDir>
    <RefGameDirCacheFile Condition="'$(LOCALAPPDATA)' != ''">$(LOCALAPPDATA)\GlobalConversationTracker\reference-game-dir.txt</RefGameDirCacheFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <_CachedGameDir Condition="'$(RefGameDirCacheFile)' != '' and Exists('$(RefGameDirCacheFile)')">$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText('$(RefGameDirCacheFile)').Trim())</_CachedGameDir>
    <DiscoElysiumDir Condition="'$(DiscoElysiumDir)' == '' and '$(DISCO_ELYSIUM_DIR)' != ''">$(DISCO_ELYSIUM_DIR)</DiscoElysiumDir>
    <DiscoElysiumDir Condition="'$(DiscoElysiumDir)' == '' and Exists('$(RepoDefaultGameDir)\BepInEx\core')">$(RepoDefaultGameDir)</DiscoElysiumDir>
    <DiscoElysiumDir Condition="'$(DiscoElysiumDir)' == '' and '$(_CachedGameDir)' != '' and Exists('$(_CachedGameDir)\BepInEx\core')">$(_CachedGameDir)</DiscoElysiumDir>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The two halves of what a build references, which come from different places
    and are worth keeping straight:

      BepInEx\core     BepInEx itself, Harmony, Il2CppInterop. Published as an
                       archive, identical on every machine, so it can simply be
                       DOWNLOADED - see the EnsureBepInExCore target, which
                       fetches the pinned build into the cache the first time
                       anything needs it. Persistence and Session reference
                       nothing else, so they need no game install at all.

      BepInEx\interop  Assembly-CSharp, DialogueSystem, UnityEngine.*, and the
                       rest of the game as IL2CPP interop assemblies. GENERATED
                       on the machine, by BepInEx, out of the game's own
                       GameAssembly.dll, the first time the game is launched
                       with BepInEx installed. There is nowhere to download
                       these from and no copy that is right for every game
                       build, which is why the plugin - and only the plugin -
                       still needs a real install.

    An install that has core is preferred over the download, so that a build
    references the same BepInEx the game beside it is running rather than two
    versions that merely happen to be pinned together.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BepInExCoreDir Condition="'$(DiscoElysiumDir)' != '' and Exists('$(DiscoElysiumDir)\BepInEx\core')">$(DiscoElysiumDir)\BepInEx\core\</BepInExCoreDir>
    <BepInExCoreDir Condition="'$(BepInExCoreDir)' == '' and '$(BepInExUnpackedDir)' != ''">$(BepInExUnpackedDir)BepInEx\core\</BepInExCoreDir>
    <BepInExInteropDir>$(DiscoElysiumDir)\BepInEx\interop\</BepInExInteropDir>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
